\b Figure Descriptions for the School Violation System (VioTrack)\b0\par
\par
\b Objectives 1, 2, and 3\b0\par
\par
Prepared for Capstone Documentation\par
\par
This part shows the pictures (Figures 25 to 45) of the main screens in the School Violation System. Each picture has a short explanation below it. The words used here are simple so that students, staff, and even ordinary people can understand what each screen does. Every description tells what the user can see on the screen, what buttons to click, and what happens after they use it.\par
\par
\b Objective 1: Recording, Storing, and Retrieving Student Violation Records\b0\par
\par
\b Figure 25. Log a Violation\b0\par
Figure 25 shows the \b Log a Violation\b0  screen of the School Violation System. This is where the admin or staff records a new violation of a student. When they open this page, they will see a big title that says \ldblquote Log a Violation\rdblquote  and a short note that says \ldblquote Record a new incident and attach necessary evidence.\rdblquote  At the top, there are three steps shown in circles: \b Student\b0 , \b Violation\b0 , and \b Details\b0 . In Step 1 (\b Student\b0 ), the user types in the search box to find the student by name, student number, or department. Each student in the list shows their full name, student number, and course or department. The user clicks on the correct student to select them, then clicks \b Next\b0  to go to Step 2. In Step 2 (\b Violation\b0 ), the user searches for the violation by code or title (for example, \ldblquote V-001\rdblquote  or the name of the offense). Each violation shows a color dot for severity (Minor, Major, or Critical) and the violation code with title. In Step 3 (\b Details\b0 ), the user fills in the \b Date & Time of Incident\b0 , the \b Witness\b0  name (if there is one), and the \b Incident Description\b0 . They can also upload picture or file proof using the upload area. Before saving, the screen shows who the offender is and what offense was picked. When the user clicks the submit button, the system saves the new case in the database with a \b Pending\b0  status. The case can now be seen in the All Cases list and can be updated later.\par
\par
\b Figure 26. Auto Sanction Preview\b0\par
Figure 26 shows the \b Auto Sanction Preview\b0  part inside Step 3 of the Log a Violation screen. After the admin picks a student and a violation, the system automatically checks how many times that student already did the same violation before. It then shows a red or rose-colored box on the screen with the words \ldblquote Expected Sanction (Offense #1)\rdblquote  or \ldblquote Offense #2\rdblquote  and so on, depending on how many times the student offended. Below that, it shows the exact sanction text that the school rules say should be given (for example, verbal warning, written warning, community service, or suspension). The admin can read this before clicking save. This way, the admin does not need to guess or compute the punishment by hand. The sanction shown follows the Rules & Regulations list in the system, so the same violation gets the same type of punishment for every student who reaches the same offense number. This helps make the discipline fair and the same for everyone.\par
\par
\b Figure 27. All Cases List\b0\par
Figure 27 shows the \b Violation Cases\b0  page, also called the All Cases List. At the top, there is a header that says \ldblquote Violation Cases\rdblquote  with a blue button \b Record Violation\b0  on the right side. Below the header, four summary boxes show numbers: \b Total Cases\b0 , \b Pending\b0 , \b Hearing Scheduled\b0 , and \b Closed Cases\b0 . These numbers update based on what is saved in the system. Under that is a search bar where the user can type a student name or violation name. There are also drop-down filters for \b Status\b0  (Pending, Endorsed, Hearing Scheduled, Closed), \b Severity\b0  (Minor, Major), \b Department\b0 , \b Academic Year\b0 , and date range (\b From\b0  and \b To\b0 ). The main part of the screen is a table with columns: \b Date / Status\b0 , \b Student\b0  (name, department, and section), \b Violation Details\b0  (violation title and severity), and \b Actions\b0  (View, Edit, Delete icons). Each row is one violation case. When a new case is saved from Log a Violation, it appears here automatically. The admin uses this page every day to check open cases, find old cases, and open a case for more details.\par
\par
\b Figure 28. Case Details\b0\par
Figure 28 shows the \b Case Details\b0  screen for one violation case. The page title shows \ldblquote Violation Case #0001\rdblquote  (the number changes per case) and the student name labeled as \ldblquote Student Respondent.\rdblquote  At the top right, there are \b Edit\b0  and \b Print\b0  buttons. A progress bar in the middle shows where the case is in the process: \b Pending\b0 , \b Hearing Scheduled\b0 , \b Sanction Served\b0 , or \b Closed\b0 . If the case was sent to the grievance committee, it shows \ldblquote Endorsed to Grievance Committee\rdblquote  instead. The main card shows the violation title, category, violation code, and the current status badge (color-coded). Below that is the full \b Incident Description\b0  written by the admin. It also shows the \b Date & Time\b0  of the incident and the \b Witnesses\b0  name, or \ldblquote No Witness Logged\rdblquote  if empty. There is an \b Assigned Sanction\b0  box that shows the offense level and the sanction given. On the side, the screen shows the student photo or initials, student number, department, section, and year level. There is also a section for \b Attached Evidence\b0  (uploaded files or pictures), \b Hearing Sessions\b0  linked to this case, and \b Case Actions\b0  like OSA intervention. Buttons at the bottom let the admin \b Start Hearing\b0 , \b Complete Hearing\b0 , \b Endorse to Grievance\b0 , \b Close Case\b0 , or \b Record OSA Intervention\b0 , depending on the case status. This page gives the full story of one violation from start to finish.\par
\par
\b Figure 29. Violation Catalog\b0\par
Figure 29 shows the \b Rules & Regulations\b0  page, which is the Violation Catalog of the system. The page header says \ldblquote Rules & Regulations\rdblquote  with a button \b Add Rule Category\b0 . There is a search box to find rules by keyword or code, and a \b Category\b0  drop-down to filter by type (for example, Academic, Conduct, Dress Code). The table lists every violation rule with these columns: \b Violation Rule / Code\b0  (shows the code like V-001 and the full title), \b Category Class\b0 , \b Severity Level\b0  (Minor Severity or Major Severity with color badges), and \b Actions\b0  (Edit and Delete). Each rule in this list is what the admin picks when logging a violation. The system uses this list so that all staff record violations using the same names, codes, and severity levels. When the school adds or changes a rule here, it right away affects new cases recorded through Log a Violation. This keeps all violation records clean, standard, and easy to compare in reports.\par
\par
\b Figure 30. Student Import\b0\par
Figure 30 shows the \b Import Students\b0  page. The title says \ldblquote Import Students\rdblquote  and below it says \ldblquote Upload a CSV or Excel file to bulk import students.\rdblquote  The user sees a big upload area where they can drag and drop a file or click to choose a file from their computer. The file must be in CSV or Excel format (.csv, .xlsx). There is also a \b Download Template\b0  link so the admin can get the correct column headers before preparing the file. The template includes columns like student name, student number, email, department, year level, section, guardian name, and guardian contact. After the user selects a file and clicks upload, a progress bar shows how much of the file has been uploaded. When done, the system reads each row and saves the students into the database. If some rows have errors (wrong format or missing data), the system shows which rows failed. After a successful import, those students appear in the Students list and can be searched when logging a violation. This saves time compared to typing each student one by one.\par
\par
\b Figure 31. Record Retrieval\b0\par
Figure 31 shows the \b Record Retrieval\b0  page under Reports. The page title is \ldblquote Search Records\rdblquote  and the description says the user can \ldblquote Search through all past records with advanced, multi-parameter filters.\rdblquote  At the top is a search box for \b student name or ID\b0 . Below are filters for \b Department\b0 , \b Academic Year\b0 , \b Violation Type\b0  (pick from the violation list), \b Severity\b0  (Minor or Major), \b Date From\b0 , \b Date To\b0 , and \b Month\b0 . The user can also \b Save Preset\b0  to remember a filter setup and \b Load Preset\b0  to use it again later. When filters are applied, the table below shows matching cases with the student name, violation, date, department, and status. Each row has \b View\b0  and \b Print\b0  buttons. There is a \b Clear Filters\b0  button to reset everything. This page is used when the admin needs to find old records fast, like when a dean asks \ldblquote Show me all Major violations in BSIT this school year\rdblquote  or when preparing documents for a hearing. The system searches the database and shows only the records that match what was filtered.\par
\par
\b Objective 2: Improving Case Management and Monitoring\b0\par
\par
\b Figure 32. Dashboard Overview\b0\par
Figure 32 shows the main \b Dashboard\b0  screen that appears after the admin logs in. At the top, four big cards show important numbers: \b Total Students\b0  (how many students are in the system), \b Violation Cases\b0  (total cases recorded), \b Active Cases\b0  (cases that are not closed yet), and \b Hearings\b0  (hearings scheduled this month). Each card can be clicked to go to the related page. Below the cards, there are charts and graphs. One chart shows \b Violation Trends\b0  per month (how many incidents happened each month). Another shows cases per department (which course or college has more violations). There is also a chart for cases by severity (Minor vs Major). A list of \b Recent Cases\b0  shows the latest violations with student name, violation title, date, and status badge. A \b Top Violations\b0  section lists the most common offenses. The admin can also filter by \b Academic Year\b0  to see data for a specific school year. This dashboard gives a quick picture of what is happening in the school without opening each case one by one. It helps the OSA or dean see problems early, like if violations are going up in one department.\par
\par
\b Figure 33. Record Violation Step 2: Select Violation\b0\par
Figure 33 shows \b Step 2: Violation\b0  inside the Log a Violation process. The step indicator at the top shows that Step 1 (Student) is done and Step 2 (Violation) is now active. The heading says \ldblquote Select Violation\rdblquote  with a search box that says \ldblquote Search violation code or title...\rdblquote  The admin types here to find the right offense quickly. The list below shows every violation from the Rules & Regulations catalog. Each item displays the violation code and title (example: \ldblquote V-003 - Improper Uniform\rdblquote ), a colored dot (yellow for Minor, orange for Major, red for Critical), and a severity badge on the right. The admin clicks the correct violation to highlight it with a red border, then clicks \b Next\b0  to go to Step 3. This step is important because picking the wrong violation will give the wrong sanction and wrong report. By searching and selecting from the official list, the admin makes sure the case is recorded under the correct school rule before entering the incident details and seeing the auto sanction preview.\par
\par
\b Figure 34. Edit Case\b0\par
Figure 34 shows the \b Edit Case\b0  screen. The page title says \ldblquote Edit Case #\rdblquote  followed by the case number. A note below says \ldblquote Update incident details. Status changes via hearings and case actions.\rdblquote  This means the admin can fix the written details here, but changing the case status (like Pending to Closed) is done through hearings and action buttons on the Case Details page, not on this edit form. The form shows read-only info about the student name and violation title so the admin knows which case they are editing. The editable fields are \b Date & Time of Incident\b0 , \b Witness\b0 , and \b Incident Description\b0 . At the bottom, there is a \b Save Changes\b0  button and a red \b Move to Trash\b0  button. When saved, the updated information is stored in the database and shows right away on the Case Details page. This is used when the admin made a typo, forgot to add witness name, or needs to add more details to the incident story. Closed cases cannot be edited from this screen.\par
\par
\b Figure 35. Student Profile with Violations\b0\par
Figure 35 shows the \b Student Profile\b0  page for one student. The header shows the student\lquote s full name, department, year level, and section. Buttons at the top include \b Print\b0 , \b Edit\b0 , \b Send Message\b0  (to guardian via SMS or email), and \b Log Violation\b0  (to record a new case for this student). A risk badge shows if the student is \b Low\b0 , \b Medium\b0 , or \b High\b0  risk based on how many violations they have (2 or more is Medium, 5 or more is High). The profile section shows contact details: email, phone, guardian name, and guardian phone. Below that is a \b Violation History\b0  table listing all cases linked to this student. Each row shows the date, violation code, violation name, severity, status (Pending, Hearing Scheduled, Closed, or Endorsed to Grievance), and a \b View\b0  button to open the full case. An offense summary shows the total number of violations. This page lets the admin see the whole behavior record of one student in one place. It is useful during counseling, parent meetings, or when deciding if a repeat offender needs a hearing.\par
\par
\b Figure 36. Cases Search and Filter\b0\par
Figure 36 shows the \b Search and Filter\b0  section on the Violation Cases page. The search bar lets the admin type a student name or violation name. As they type, the list below updates after a short delay (about 300 milliseconds) without reloading the whole page. The filter drop-downs include: \b Status\b0  with choices All Statuses, Pending, Endorsed, Hearing Scheduled, and Closed; \b Severity\b0  with All Severities, Minor, and Major; \b Department\b0  with all school departments like BSIT, BSCS, and others; \b Academic Year\b0  like 2024-2025; and \b From\b0  / \b To\b0  date pickers for a date range. A clear button resets all filters back to show every case. When filters are active, only matching rows stay in the table. For example, if the admin picks Status = Pending and Department = BSIT, only open BSIT cases will show. This saves time when there are hundreds of cases in the system and the admin only needs to work on a specific group, like all Major violations this month or all pending cases in one department.\par
\par
\b Figure 37. Students Search\b0\par
Figure 37 shows the \b Students\b0  list page with its search and filter tools. At the top, summary cards may show total students and other counts. The search box lets the admin type a student\lquote s name, student number, or email. Filters include \b Department\b0  (course or college), \b Year Level\b0  (1st Year, 2nd Year, 3rd Year, 4th Year), and \b Academic Year\b0 . Buttons at the top include \b Add Student\b0 , \b Import Students\b0 , \b Promote Year Levels\b0 , and \b Graduate 4th Years\b0 . The table shows each student\lquote s name, student number, department, year level, section, and action buttons (\b View\b0 , \b Edit\b0 , \b Delete\b0 ). When the admin searches, only students matching the typed words appear. This page is the starting point before opening a student profile or logging a violation. Instead of scrolling through thousands of names, the admin types a few letters and finds the student in seconds.\par
\par
\b Figure 38. Advanced Record Retrieval\b0\par
Figure 38 shows the \b Advanced Record Retrieval\b0  filters on the Search Records page under Reports. This is a more detailed version of basic search. The admin can combine many filters at the same time: search by student name or ID, pick a specific \b Violation Type\b0  from the drop-down list, filter by \b Department\b0 , choose an \b Academic Year\b0 , set \b Severity\b0  to Minor or Major, and set a date range using \b Date From\b0 , \b Date To\b0 , or pick a whole \b Month\b0 . The \b Save Preset\b0  feature lets the admin name and save a favorite filter setup (for example, \ldblquote BSIT Major 2025\rdblquote ) in the browser. \b Load Preset\b0  brings it back with one click. Results appear in a table with student info, violation details, date, and status. Each result can be viewed or printed. This is especially helpful at the end of the semester or school year when the school needs a list of all violations in a department, or when preparing evidence for a grievance hearing. The system reads all saved cases and shows only what matches every filter chosen.\par
\par
\b Objective 3: Reports, Analytics, and Decision-Making\b0\par
\par
\b Figure 39. System Reports and Trends\b0\par
Figure 39 shows the \b System Reports\b0  page. The title says \ldblquote System Reports\rdblquote  with a note: \ldblquote Overview statistics \emdash  total cases, status breakdown & department analysis.\rdblquote  Five stat cards at the top show: \b Total Cases\b0 , \b Pending\b0 , \b Hearing\b0 , \b Endorsed\b0 , and \b Closed\b0  \emdash  each with a number and icon. Below that, a line chart titled with the current year shows monthly trends for \b Minor\b0  and \b Major\b0  violations from January to December. A pie chart shows the breakdown of cases by status (how many are still pending, in hearing, endorsed, or closed). Another section lists \b Top Violations\b0  \emdash  the most recorded offenses in the school. A \b Cases by Department\b0  chart or table shows which departments (BSIT, BSCS, etc.) have the most cases. The date stamp \ldblquote As of [today\lquote s date]\rdblquote  appears on the page. This report helps school officials answer questions like: Are violations going up or down? Which month had the most cases? Which department needs more attention? The numbers come directly from all cases saved in the system.\par
\par
\b Figure 40. Department Top Violations\b0\par
Figure 40 shows the \b Department Top Violations\b0  report, which can be seen on the Dashboard or System Reports page. This report groups all violation cases by the student\lquote s department (course or college). For each department, it shows which violations happen most often and how many times each one was recorded. For example, it might show that BSIT has 15 cases of \ldblquote Late Attendance\rdblquote  and 8 cases of \ldblquote Improper Uniform,\rdblquote  while BSCS has more \ldblquote Phone Use in Class\rdblquote  cases. The data is shown in a chart or ranked list so the admin can quickly see which department has the highest number of cases and what the common problems are. School officials use this to plan seminars, reminders, or stricter monitoring in departments with many violations. It also helps deans of each college see their own department\lquote s situation without looking at other departments\lquote  data.\par
\par
\b Figure 41. Sanctions Compliance\b0\par
Figure 41 shows the \b Sanctions Report\b0  page. The header says \ldblquote Sanctions Report\rdblquote  with the description: \ldblquote Track imposed sanctions, compliance status, and sanction outcomes per student.\rdblquote  Four summary cards show: \b Total Sanctions\b0  (how many sanctions were given), \b Sanction Served\b0  (how many students already finished their punishment), \b Pending Sanction\b0  (how many are not yet done), and \b Compliance Rate\b0  (shown as a percentage). A green progress bar labeled \ldblquote Overall Sanction Compliance\rdblquote  shows visually how many sanctions are completed. Filters let the admin narrow results by \b Department\b0 , \b Severity\b0 , \b Sanction Status\b0  (Served or Pending), and \b Date Range\b0 . The table lists each case with student name, violation, sanction given, and whether the sanction is served or still pending. This page answers an important question: Did the student actually do the punishment that was given? If many sanctions are still pending, the admin knows they need to follow up. A high compliance rate means the discipline process is working. A low rate means many students have not completed their sanctions yet.\par
\par
\b Figure 42. Violation Ledger and Exports\b0\par
Figure 42 shows the \b Reports & Analytics\b0  page, which works as the Violation Ledger. The page title is \ldblquote Reports & Analytics\rdblquote  and it lists all violation cases in a table with columns: \b Date & Timestamp\b0 , \b Student Profile\b0  (name and department), \b Violation Details\b0  (title and category), \b Lifecycle Status\b0  (Pending, Hearing Scheduled, Endorsed, Closed), and action buttons. At the top right, three export buttons are visible: \b Export CSV\b0  (downloads a spreadsheet file), \b Export PDF\b0  (downloads a PDF document), and \b Print Report\b0  (opens a print-ready page). Search and filters let the admin pick a student name, department, and case status before exporting. This means the admin can export only the records they need, not the whole database. The exported file can be printed, emailed, or filed for school records, accreditation, or meetings with the dean. This replaces the old way of copying data by hand into Excel or typing reports from memory.\par
\par
\b Figure 43. Dashboard Violation Trends\b0\par
Figure 43 shows the \b Violation Trends\b0  chart on the Dashboard. This is a line graph that shows how many violation cases were recorded each month. The horizontal line (X-axis) shows the months (Jan, Feb, Mar, and so on). The vertical line (Y-axis) shows the number of cases. Each point on the line represents the total incidents in that month. When the line goes up, it means more violations happened that month. When it goes down, fewer violations were recorded. The admin can move the mouse over each point to see the exact number. This chart updates automatically whenever a new case is saved or a case is closed. School officials use this to spot patterns, like more violations during exam week or at the start of the school year. Instead of counting cases manually from a notebook, they can see the trend in one picture on the dashboard.\par
\par
\b Figure 44. Printable Violation Report\b0\par
Figure 44 shows the \b Printable Violation Report\b0  for a single case. When the admin clicks the \b Print\b0  button on the Case Details page, this formal document opens in a new tab. The header shows the school name (I-Link CST Violation System), the title \ldblquote STUDENT DISCIPLINARY RECORD\rdblquote  or similar, and the case number. The report is divided into sections: \b I. Student Information\b0  (full name, student number, department, year level, section), \b II. Violation Details\b0  (violation code, title, category, severity, date and time, witness, and full description), and \b III. Sanctions & Interventions\b0  (offense level, assigned sanction, and any actions taken). The layout uses a clean black-and-white format fit for A4 paper. A \b Print Report\b0  button at the top triggers the browser print dialog. This printed copy can be signed, filed in the student\lquote s folder, attached to a hearing packet, or given to the guardian during a meeting. It serves as the official paper record of one violation case.\par
\par
\b Figure 45. Student Violation Summary\b0\par
Figure 45 shows the \b Student Violation Summary\b0  printout from the Student Profile page. When the admin clicks \b Print\b0  on a student\lquote s profile, the system generates a document titled \ldblquote STUDENT VIOLATION RECORD.\rdblquote  The header shows I-Link CST Violation System, the label \ldblquote Official Record,\rdblquote  and the date and time it was generated. The \b Student Details\b0  section lists the student\lquote s full name, ID or email, year and section, department, and guardian name with phone number. The \b Violation History\b0  section is a table with columns: \b Date\b0 , \b Code\b0 , \b Violation\b0 , \b Description\b0 , \b Witness\b0 , and \b Action/Status\b0 . Every case linked to that student appears in the table, from the oldest to the newest. If the student has no violations, it says \ldblquote No violation records found.\rdblquote  This summary is used when the school needs the complete behavior history of one student in one document \emdash  for counseling, parent conference, dean\lquote s review, or grievance committee hearing. The admin does not need to open each case one by one and copy the details. The system gathers everything automatically and puts it in one printable report.\par
\par
\b Summary\b0\par
Figures 25 to 45 show the main parts of the School Violation System (VioTrack) based on the three objectives of the study. Figure 25 to 31 cover Objective 1: recording new violations step by step, saving them in the database, and finding old records through search and filters. Figure 32 to 38 cover Objective 2: monitoring cases through the dashboard, editing case details, viewing student profiles with full violation history, and using advanced filters to manage many records. Figure 39 to 45 cover Objective 3: generating reports, checking if sanctions were served, exporting data to CSV and PDF, reading violation trends on charts, and printing official documents for school use. Together, these screens show that the system helps the school record violations properly, track each case from Pending to Closed, and produce reports that school officials can use to make better decisions about student discipline.\par
}
